# Never commit keystores or secrets
#
# The iOS half was missing. `git check-ignore` answered NO for
# ios/Runner/dist.mobileprovision, ios/Runner/dist.cer, ios/certs/dist.pfx and
# android/app/x.pem, so .github/scripts/check-signing-material.sh could only
# catch those AFTER they were committed -- and by then the key is public. These
# patterns keep them out of the index in the first place. Measured: zero
# tracked files match any of them (`git ls-files | grep -iE
# '\.(pem|key|cer|p12|pfx|mobileprovision|jks|keystore)$'` returns nothing),
# so nothing legitimate becomes ignored.
android/key.properties
**/key.properties
**/*.keystore
**/*.jks
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/*.mobileprovision
**/*.cer
**/*.pem
**/*.key
#**/*.env
#.env

# Coverage
coverage/

# Flutter writes golden comparison output here on a failed run. Never committed: it is a
# diff of a candidate against a baseline, not a baseline, and it is regenerated every run.
test/golden/failures/

# Captured golden candidates. IGNORED ON PURPOSE, and the purpose is an owner instruction:
# "The eight captured golden images are candidates, not approved baselines... Do not
# automatically commit screenshots as approved baselines."
#
# A capture run writes eight PNGs here. Untracked-but-not-ignored, one `git add -A` would have
# committed them, and a committed file in this directory IS the baseline every later run asserts
# against -- so the approval would have happened by accident, in a commit about something else.
# Ignoring them makes approval a deliberate act instead.
#
# The candidates for review are committed under docs/evidence/route-1-candidates/ -- a contact
# sheet plus the eight originals -- which is what a human reviews. When a baseline is APPROVED,
# it is added explicitly:
#
#     git add -f test/golden/goldens/<surface>.<theme>-<dir>.png
#
# Until then each golden case SKIPS and says how to capture it (customer_card_golden_test.dart),
# so an absent baseline asserts nothing rather than passing vacuously.
test/golden/goldens/*.png

# =================================================================================================
# DATABASE DUMPS AND ARCHIVES -- recurrence containment for P1-SEC-40 (issue #120). 2026-11-11.
# =================================================================================================
#
# This repository is clean: no dump-shaped file is tracked, and none appears anywhere in its
# history (checked with `git rev-list --objects --all`). These patterns are prevention, not
# remediation. The sibling repository, syriastore98, had a 20 MB production dump committed to its
# default branch for 18 days; the full incident record is in that repository at
# docs/security/P1-SEC-40-INCIDENT.md. This block exists so the same mistake cannot land here.
#
# On the Firebase config files that are committed here deliberately --
# android/app/google-services.json and ios/Runner/GoogleService-Info.plist each carry one
# AIza-prefixed Google API key. Those are client-side credentials by Google's own design: they
# ship inside every released APK and IPA and are extractable from any published build, so
# committing them discloses nothing a shipped app does not. They are NOT part of the incident and
# they do not need rotating on its account. What they DO need is restriction in the Google Cloud
# console -- by Android package name plus signing SHA-1, by iOS bundle id, and by the specific
# APIs each key may call -- or an unrestricted key invites billing abuse. Verify that separately.
*.sql
*.sql.gz
*.dump
*.bak
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
